th prereleases=True for each spec: >=1 contains 1.5a1 (prereleases=True) yes; <2 yes; !=1.5a1: does contains(parsed, prereleases=True) exclude 1.5a1? Specifier '!=' with contains — 1.5a1 == 1.5a1 so excluded. Good, skipped. 1.6rc1: all contain → pending. 3: skipped by <2. found_final False → yield pending → ['1.6rc1']. Good.\n\ncheck('>1.0,<2', ['1.0.post1','1.1a1']) → 1.0.post1: post release, is_prerelease False → yielded, found_final=True. 1.1a1 pending but suppressed. Expected ['1.1a1']! Wait — expected ['1.1a1'] but 1.0.post1 would be yielded by this code? Let me check: `>1.0` — does 1.0.post1 satisfy >1.0? Yes, 1.0.post1 > 1.0. So code yields 1.0.post1 and suppresses 1.1a1 → result ['1.0.post1'], but grader expects ['1.1a1'].\n\nHmm wait, let me re-check. `check('>1.0,<2', ['1.0.post1', '1.1a1'], ['1.1a1'])`. Grader expects only '1.1a1'. But the reference code would yield 1.0.post1 (it's a final/post release, not prerelease, passes >1.0 and <2). So reference yields ['1.0.post1'] and grader expects ['1.1a1'] — that's a contradiction... but wait, is this a grader assertion contradicting the requirement? The requirement says \"when no matching final or post release exists, return matching prereleases\". Here a matching post release 1.0.post1 exists, so per requirement, prereleases should be suppressed and the post release returned → ['1.0.post1']. But grader expects ['1.1a1'].\n\nHmm, unless... the grader's expectation is wrong relative to requirement? Or maybe the semantics intend that the filter compares per-spec prerelease containment... Wait, but `>1.0` with default prereleases — Specifier.contains('1.0.post1')... 1.0.post1 is not a prerelease. >1.0 satisfied. So it's yielded.\n\nHold on, maybe the requirement intends \"matching final\" differently? Actually wait — maybe the intended semantic is the standard packaging one: prereleases are only included if a prerelease is allowed... Actually in real packaging, SpecifierSet.filter returns items passing all specs